      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">417</data>
      <data key="d13">450</data>
      <data key="d14">class _HTTPSOnlyRedirect(urllib.request.HTTPRedirectHandler):</data>
      <data key="d15">Refuse any redirect hop that leaves https.

    The scheme check in `_fetch_json` covers the URL it is handed and nothing
    after it: `urlopen` follows redirects itself, and CPython's
    `HTTPRedirectHandler` accepts targets whose scheme is in
    `('http', 'https', 'ftp', '')`. So an issuer -- or anyone who can answer
    for it -- returns a `302` from the discovery document or the `jwks_uri` to
    `http://`, and the signing keys every authentication decision rests on
    travel in clear. That is not only disclosure: an on-path attacker who
    answers the downgraded request substitutes t</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="392.85" y="455.35" width="130.07" height="27.95" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">_HTTPSOnlyRedi…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
