    <node id="sym:tests/test_http_transport.py::test_iss197_a_withheld_request_body_is_answered_rather_than_waited_on">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_http_transport.py::test_iss197_a_withheld_request_body_is_answered_rather_than_waited_on</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">test_iss197_a_withheld_request_body_is_answered_rather_than_waited_on</data>
      <data key="d10">test_iss197_a_withheld_request_body_is_answered_rather_than_waited_on</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_http_transport.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">820</data>
      <data key="d13">847</data>
      <data key="d14">def test_iss197_a_withheld_request_body_is_answered_rather_than_waited_on(make_server, monkeypatch):</data>
      <data key="d15">Issue 197: headers promising a megabyte, then silence.

    Without a handler `timeout`, socketserver never arms settimeout() and
    `rfile.read(Content-Length)` blocks for as long as the client cares to hold
    the socket -- one pinned thread per connection, on a server that caps
    neither. The shipped 30s is far too long to sit through here, so the test
    pins the attribute's existence separately and then shrinks it to prove the
    second half: that the read failing becomes a 408 rather than a handler
    thread dying with the client still waiting.</data>
      <data key="d16">true</data>
      <data key="d17">20</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="1748.60" y="2848.45" width="145.20" height="31.20" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">test_iss197_a_…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
    <node id="sym:tests/test_http_transport.py::test_iss197_a_body_shorter_than_content_length_is_408_not_a_hang">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_http_transport.py::test_iss197_a_body_shorter_than_content_length_is_408_not_a_hang</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">test_iss197_a_body_shorter_than_content_length_is_408_not_a_hang</data>
      <data key="d10">test_iss197_a_body_shorter_than_content_length_is_408_not_a_hang</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_http_transport.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">850</data>
      <data key="d13">873</data>
      <data key="d14">def test_iss197_a_body_shorter_than_content_length_is_408_not_a_hang(make_server, monkeypatch):</data>
      <data key="d15">The polite version of the same thing: the client closes early.

    `rfile.read(length)` returns short at EOF rather than raising, so this path
    never reaches the timeout at all -- it has to be noticed by comparing what
    arrived against what was promised.</data>
      <data key="d16">true</data>
      <data key="d17">12</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="1917.80" y="2848.45" width="160.32" height="34.45" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">test_iss197_a_…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
    <node id="sym:tests/test_http_transport.py::AbortingWriter">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_http_transport.py::AbortingWriter</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">AbortingWriter</data>
      <data key="d10">AbortingWriter</data>
      <data key="d11">class</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_http_transport.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">876</data>
      <data key="d13">906</data>
      <data key="d14">class AbortingWriter:</data>
      <data key="d15">A `wfile` that raises ConnectionAbortedError from the nth write on.

    The real shape is a client that hangs up mid-response, which Windows
    reports as ConnectionAbortedError -- the sibling of BrokenPipeError and
    ConnectionResetError that the old guard did not name. Forcing it beats
    racing a real socket close, which lands on a different write every run.

    Write 1 is the header flush from `end_headers()`; write 2 is the body.</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
